# The national unit for usa. Items take n=1; the world items reviewed here change by level.
# Dates are in engine/notation/chronicle.ts with their sources.
lesson history-g6-usa-how-our-country-is-governed v=1 format=teach grade=6 unit=9 subject=history nation=usa levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "How the United States is governed"
  goal "Put the founding of the United States' government in order, and say why its Constitution splits power three ways."

  look {
    scene 36x9 {
      timeline line from=1760 to=1940 step=30 years=[1776, 1787, 1920] labels=["Declaration of Independence", "Constitution written", "Women vote in the United States"] at=canvas(0, 0)
    }
    say """
      In 1776 the colonies declared their independence. In 1787 their leaders wrote the Constitution, which shares power between Congress, which makes the laws, the President, who carries them out, and the courts, which judge by them. In 1791 the Bill of Rights added the people's freedoms, and in 1920 women across the country won the vote.
      """
  }
  do {
    level easy {
      worked history.n6-what n=1
    }
    show history.n6-order n=1 k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    show history.n6-between n=1 k=0 hard-k=1
    show history.n6-century n=1 k=0 hard-k=1
    show history.law-steps k=2 easy-k=0 hard-k=4
    show history.vote-order k=2 easy-k=0 hard-k=4
    show history.un-order k=2 easy-k=0 hard-k=4
    show history.n6-order n=1 k=2 easy-k=1 hard-k=0
    level medium hard {
      show history.n6-what n=1
    }
    level easy {
      show history.n6-century n=1 k=1
    }
    level hard {
      show history.law-steps k=5
    }
  }
  story {
    show history.n6-between n=1 k=1 hard-k=0
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show history.n6-why n=1
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show history.n6-find-out n=1
  }
  remember {
    say "Every date in our country's story can be put on the line and counted from, and every one came from a source."
  }

  grown-ups """
    The idea is how the family's own country is governed today and how it came to be, told as
    facts and without verdicts. The way in names what a thing was; the core orders three
    events, counts the years between two, names a century, and reviews how laws are made, votes and
    the United Nations; the two-star question asks why something was done. If the child is stuck on
    counting, count to the next hundred first. The three-star task is for you to mark: a small
    piece of finding out with you. State standards and the C3 Framework ask children in the upper grades to learn how the Constitution divides power (recalled, not quoted here).
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level a worked example says what the thing was, the events come nearly in order, the years counted are the closest pair, and the review questions are the plainest."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the events are fully muddled, the years counted are the longer pair, the century asked has a trap, and the review questions cross year one."
  }
}
